<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarikh al-Fattash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tarikh al-Fattash is an Arabic chronicle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">western Sudan</a> written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, traditionally attributed to Mahmud Kati and completed by a descendant, Ibn al-Mukhtar, in the seventeenth century. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-sudan">Tarikh al-Sudan</a> it is the main written source for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a> under the Askias and for the Moroccan conquest of 1591; parts of the surviving text were altered in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarikh-al-fattash.jpg" alt="Tarikh al-Fattash" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ta%27rikh_al-Fattash_Mamma_Haidara_Library_34122_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
